  "answer_text": "Making eye contact with someone who has a \"lazy eye\" (physically known as **strabismus**, where the eyes do not align in the same direction) can sometimes feel socially tricky. You want to be respectful and attentive, but you might worry about looking at the \"wrong\" eye or making them feel self-conscious. \n\nThe most respectful, natural ways to handle eye contact in this situation include:\n\n### 1. Look at the \"dominant\" eye\nUsually, someone with strabismus will use one dominant eye to focus on you, while the other eye drifts slightly. \n* **What to do:** Look at the eye that is pointing directly at you. This is the eye they are actively using to look at you, and focusing on it will feel like natural, direct eye contact to both of you.\n\n### 2. The \"Bridge of the Nose\" Trick\nIf you find it difficult to figure out which eye is focusing on you, or if you find your eyes naturally drifting toward the misaligned eye, look at the **bridge of their nose** (right between their eyes).\n* **Why it works:** To the other person, looking at the bridge of their nose is visually indistinguishable from looking them straight in the eyes. It relieves you of the pressure of choosing an eye, prevents you from staring at the misaligned eye, and makes them feel fully seen and heard.\n* **Alternative:** You can also look at their brow line or the center of their forehead for the same effect. \n\n### 3. Avoid \"eye-darting\"\nWhen people are unsure where to look, their eyes tend to dart rapidly back and forth between the other person's left eye and right eye. \n* **Why to avoid it:** People with strabismus are highly attuned to this. They can easily see your eyes flicking back and forth as you try to process their misalignment, which often makes them feel highly self-conscious and may cause them to look away. Pick a spot (the dominant eye or the bridge of the nose) and commit to it.\n\n### 4. Remember: They think they are looking right at you\nEven though one of their eyes is pointing in a different direction, their brain is generally ignoring the input from the misaligned eye to avoid double vision. In their mind, they are looking directly at you. \n* **What to do:** Don't look behind you to see what they are \"looking at,\" and don't pause the conversation because you think they are distracted. Treat the interaction exactly as you would with anyone else.\n\n### 5. Keep a natural conversational pace\nEven with perfect vision, staring intensely at someone without breaking eye contact can feel aggressive or uncomfortable. \n* **What to do:** Use normal conversational eye contact. It is completely natural to look away briefly when you are thinking, speaking, or gesturing, and then bring your focus back to their face when they are speaking. \n\nUltimately, the best approach is to focus on **what they are saying** rather than where they are looking. People with strabismus appreciate when you normalize the interaction and treat them with the same relaxed engagement you would offer anyone else.",
